1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,675
Welcome to the Singapore Haze Observatory.

2
00:00:02,675 --> 00:00:09,808
This walkthrough explores regional air quality and acute respiratory infection visits
using the two supplied datasets.

3
00:00:09,808 --> 00:00:13,820
Use the left sidebar to select regions and dates.

4
00:00:13,820 --> 00:00:20,062
The three tabs cover regional haze, polyclinic associations, and the evidence behind
the analysis.

5
00:00:21,062 --> 00:00:22,726
Start with Regional haze.

6
00:00:22,726 --> 00:00:27,716
The lines track PSI in North, South, East, West, and Central Singapore.

7
00:00:27,716 --> 00:00:31,875
The dashed line marks the unhealthy threshold, above one hundred.

8
00:00:31,875 --> 00:00:35,619
The highest reading is one hundred and ninety two.

9
00:00:35,619 --> 00:00:39,778
No region reaches the very unhealthy band in this dataset.

10
00:00:40,778 --> 00:00:46,226
Switch to Daily averages to make the broad pattern easier to see.

11
00:00:46,226 --> 00:00:51,221
September shows intermittent increases, followed by sustained deterioration in early
October.

12
00:00:51,221 --> 00:00:55,762
Averages here are analytical summaries of the supplied PSI readings.

13
00:00:55,762 --> 00:01:01,664
Importantly, the clinic series ends on October third, before the strongest later
episode.

14
00:01:02,664 --> 00:01:05,885
The heatmap and exposure table compare regions.

15
00:01:05,885 --> 00:01:14,628
Central has the highest mean PSI, at about one hundred and one point five, followed
by West and East.

16
00:01:14,628 --> 00:01:17,849
Similar rises across regions suggest widespread pollution.

17
00:01:17,849 --> 00:01:24,291
But PSI alone cannot identify a transboundary smoke source, wind direction, or
responsible pollutant.

18
00:01:25,291 --> 00:01:26,697
Open Polyclinic association.

19
00:01:26,697 --> 00:01:32,789
These are average daily acute respiratory infection visits, rather than all clinic
attendances.

20
00:01:32,789 --> 00:01:35,601
Four adequately covered weeks are compared.

21
00:01:35,601 --> 00:01:40,757
Pearson correlation is zero point six one one, a positive association.

22
00:01:40,757 --> 00:01:47,318
The p value is zero point three eight nine, so the evidence remains inconclusive.

23
00:01:48,318 --> 00:01:51,392
Each scatter point represents one clinic week.

24
00:01:51,392 --> 00:01:56,223
A handful of points makes the estimate sensitive to individual weeks.

25
00:01:56,223 --> 00:02:01,932
The timing selector lets you compare previous week PSI with current week visits.

26
00:02:01,932 --> 00:02:09,398
These exploratory checks cannot establish causation, and national clinic counts
cannot reveal which regions' residents were affected.

27
00:02:10,398 --> 00:02:14,123
The alignment audit shows which exposure weeks are eligible.

28
00:02:14,123 --> 00:02:19,918
One clinic week has no PSI overlap, and another has only partial date coverage.

29
00:02:19,918 --> 00:02:25,713
The primary analysis requires full calendar overlap and at least ninety percent
hourly coverage.

30
00:02:25,713 --> 00:02:31,095
Use Download weekly analysis to save the aligned table as a CSV file.

31
00:02:32,095 --> 00:02:37,889
Finally, Data and methodology documents missing readings, assumptions, and official
interpretation references.

32
00:02:37,889 --> 00:02:43,684
Every midnight reading is absent, with two further missing hours in September.

33
00:02:43,684 --> 00:02:48,031
Infection trends and care seeking can also change visits.

34
00:02:48,031 --> 00:02:57,206
The takeaway is worsening regional air quality, with a small, inconclusive clinic
association that warrants longer, better aligned data.
